1899 0 obj
  << /ActualText (In disaggregating the total flow signal\054 we focused on four distinct hydrologic processes\072 diurnal variation\054 long\055term seasonal variation\054 slow response rainfall derived infiltration and inflow \050RDI\057I\051\054 and fast response RDI\057I\056 First\054 we removed the diurnal variation from the total flow using pattern recognition techniques\056 From there\054 we used\240 base\055flow isolation filters to identify and isolate the long\055term seasonal variation and the slow RDI\057I\056 The remaining portion of the signal was the fast RDI\057I signal\056 For base flow isolation\054 we used Lynn\055Hollick \0501979\051 filter with an alpha of 0\056925\056 We used five passes \050three forward and two backward\051 on daily flow values to isolate the seasonal variation\056 After removing the seasonal variation\054 we used five passes on hourly flow values to isolate slow RDI\057I\056 \nWe assumed the diurnal variation stays constant throughout the training\240 and modeling periods for both the traditional and disaggregated calibration approaches\056 The \240remaining sub\055signals were modeled using the Antecedent Moisture Model \050AMM\051 approach as described by Edgren\054 et\056al \0502024\051\056 For seasonal variation modeling\054 we used a four\055parameter AMM\056 We used two six\055parameter AMMs for the slow and fast RDI\057I\056 The traditional calibration approach involved calibrating all sixteen parameters simultaneously\056 The disaggregated calibration approach involved calibrating the four or six sub\055signal model parameters independently\056 \nWe used Python to run a parameter optimization algorithm based on a weighted objective function\056 Using Scikit Learn as our main machine learning library\054 we developed separate modules that streamline the process of signal disaggregation\054 model calibration\054 model prediction\054 and results presentation\056 \nFor this specific case study\054 we used hourly flow data provided by the Milwaukee Metropolitan Sewerage District\056)
     /K [ 35 ]
     /P 1900 0 R
     /Pg 1864 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

